<% content_for :title, "What Am I Escaping? | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="addiction-escaping-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-hero">
    <div class="addiction-escaping-hero-content">

      <p class="addiction-escaping-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / WHAT AM I ESCAPING?
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>WHAT</span>
        <span>AM I</span>
        <span>ESCAPING?</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="addiction-escaping-intro">
        THE PAIN. THE NOISE. THE EXHAUSTION.<br>
        WHAT DO YOU WANT TO FEEL DIFFERENT FROM?
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / OPENING -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-pink">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">01 / THE PROMISE</p>

      <h2>
        A BREAK<br>
        FROM ALL<br>
        OF THIS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <blockquote>
        “Life sucks no matter what, so don’t be fooled
        by location changes.”
        <br><br>
        — Daria
      </blockquote>

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Monitoring thoughts, managing pain, and considering every
        mood, impulse, expense, and consequence gets exhausting.
      </p>

      <p>
        Alcohol offers a familiar promise: for a little while,
        you will feel different. Ask what you want to feel
        different from.
      </p>

      <p>
        The answer can change. Pain, pleasure, habit, or restlessness
        may each play a part. Approach the question with curiosity.
        You do not need one explanation for every time you drank.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / PHYSICAL PAIN -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-yellow">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">02 / YOUR BODY</p>

      <h2>
        YOU NEED<br>
        A FUCKING<br>
        BREAK.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Pain can make your world feel smaller.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your back hurts, your leg burns, or your foot goes numb.
        Ordinary tasks require planning. You get tired of
        negotiating with your body and missing choices you
        used to take for granted.
      </p>

      <p>
        Drinking may promise temporary distance from the pain
        and its restrictions. Recognizing that promise helps
        you identify the need without assuming alcohol will
        safely meet it.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        THE NEED FOR RELIEF<br>
        DESERVES ATTENTION.
      </div>

      <p>
        Ask for practical help and use your established pain plan.
        Let your care team know when the support available
        is insufficient.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / MOOD -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-purple">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">03 / YOUR MOOD</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT ARE<br>
        YOU TRYING<br>
        TO CHANGE?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p>
        Months of depression can make you desperate for an evening
        that feels different. You want a break from the heaviness,
        emptiness, or repetition. You want to feel pleasure again.
      </p>

      <p>
        During mania, you might chase excitement or follow an
        urgent impulse. You might also want relief from racing
        thoughts and relentless energy. Ask what you were seeking
        in that particular moment.
      </p>

      <p>
        With mixed symptoms, you can feel miserable and restless,
        wanting your thoughts to stop while needing to do something.
        Notice when an immediate change starts mattering more
        than what happens tomorrow.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        WHAT DO YOU HOPE<br>
        THE DRINK WILL CHANGE?
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / SHAME -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-black">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">04 / YOUR OWN JUDGMENT</p>

      <h2>
        OFF THE<br>
        WITNESS<br>
        STAND.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p>
        You replay money spent, decisions made, and consequences
        you wish you could undo. Alcohol may promise distance
        from the voice asking how the fuck you let it happen.
        Wanting to drink can then become another source of shame.
      </p>

      <p>
        Taking responsibility does not require holding yourself
        on trial all day. Identify what needs repair, what needs
        support, and what cannot be undone.
      </p>

      <p>
        Shame turns mistakes into a verdict about who you are.
        Understanding asks what happened and what could help
        you respond differently.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        YOU CAN FACE WHAT HAPPENED<br>
        AND REMAIN WORTHY OF CARE.
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / UNCERTAINTY AND BOREDOM -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-pink">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">05 / THE UNANSWERED AND THE ORDINARY</p>

      <h2>
        TOO MANY<br>
        QUESTIONS.<br>
        NOTHING<br>
        TO DO.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p>
        Health, work, money, and the future can leave you caring
        about outcomes you cannot guarantee. Ask what action
        is available today, then allow some unanswered questions
        to stay unanswered for a while.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes you are bored. The day feels flat and you want
        something to happen. You do not need to invent a deeper
        wound to explain every urge.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ask what enjoyment, stimulation, creativity, or connection
        is missing and what you could try while protecting
        your recovery.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / EXHAUSTION -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-yellow">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">06 / TIRED OF MANAGING EVERYTHING</p>

      <h2>
        FUCK ALL<br>
        OF THIS.<br>
        JUST FOR<br>
        TONIGHT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p>
        You take your medication, monitor symptoms, work on sobriety,
        and think about consequences. A part of you may resent
        how much effort staying well requires.
      </p>

      <p>
        Listen to how tired that part is. What could help it get
        relief before it wants to abandon everything?
      </p>

      <p>
        Start with an honest sentence: “My pain is wearing me down.”
        “I feel trapped.” “I’m ashamed.” “I want excitement.”
        Naming the experience gives you somewhere to begin.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        WHAT KIND OF CARE<br>
        DOES THIS FEELING CALL FOR?
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / THE NEXT HOUR -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-purple">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">07 / BRING SUPPORT CLOSER</p>

      <h2>
        START WITH<br>
        THE NEXT<br>
        HOUR.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p>
        Some needs have a practical next step: food, company,
        help with a task, or a conversation with your care team.
        Others involve grief, uncertainty, or feelings that take
        longer to change.
      </p>

      <p>
        Choose a manageable stretch of time. What could help
        you through the next hour while protecting what you
        still care about?
      </p>

      <p>
        Ask for relief, treatment, distraction, companionship,
        and practical help. Tell someone when you start bargaining
        with yourself. You do not have to sit alone with suffering
        to prove your commitment to sobriety.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="ae-ending-small">
        KEEP RETURNING TO THE QUESTION.
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT DO<br>
        YOU NEED<br>
        <span>RIGHT NOW?</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="ae-ending-copy">

      <p>
        The answer may be pain today, shame tomorrow,
        and simple restlessness another night.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each answer helps you understand your patterns.
        What is happening inside you? What do you hope drinking
        will change? What support do you need before the urge
        becomes a decision?
      </p>

      <p>
        You can acknowledge the desire to escape and bring
        care closer at the same time.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION",
          addiction_path,
          class: "ae-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>